      "role": "user",
      "content": "that's closer, but i still feel like it's missing that real cultural \"weight\" that makes the satire bite. when you think about these neighborhoods, the tension usually comes from the fact that the locals have a completely different way of existing in the space compared to the newcomers. \n\ncan you lean harder into the linguistic differences? for the local character, use more dialect—maybe some AAVE or more specific urban slang—to really establish that they come from a different background than the \"suits\" they're talking about. and maybe make the character feel a bit more dangerous or unpredictable, like someone you wouldn't want to mess with on their own street. it'll make the contrast with the \"soft\" newcomers much funnier if the local sounds more intimidating and \"from the streets.\"",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
